the target language is: lps.

% Two agents disagreeing about the lights.
% Source: legacy_lps1/examples/CLOUT_workshop/badlight.pl
%
% The 2-D visual mapping is NOT in this language (docs/le_lps_surface.md
% §3.10): display/2 is a property list of shapes and coordinates and reads no
% better in English than in Prolog. It lives in the companion file
% badlight.lps, which compiles together with this one.

the maximum time is 12.

the actions are:
    *a person* switches the light in *a room* *a setting*; known as switch.
    *a person* goes to *a room*; known as goto.

the fluents are:
    the light in *a room* is *a setting*; known as light.
    *a person* is in *a room*; known as location.

the knowledge base badlight includes:

initially the light in livingroom is off
    and the light in kitchen is on
    and the light in bedroom is on
    and the light in bathroom is on
    and bob is in livingroom
    and dad is in kitchen.

if bob is in a room at a first time
    and the light in the room is off at the first time
then bob switches the light in the room on from the first time to a second time.

if the light in a room is on at a first time
    and dad is in the room at the first time
then dad switches the light in the room off from the first time to a second time.

if the light in a room is on at a first time
    and it is not the case that dad is in the room at the first time
then dad goes to the room from a second time to a third time.

when a person switches the light in a room a setting
then the light in the room is the setting.

when a person switches the light in a room a setting
then it is not the case that the light in the room is a second setting.

when a person goes to a room
then the person is in the room.

when a person goes to a room
then it is not the case that the person is in a second room.

it must not be true that
    dad goes to a room from a first time to a second time
    and dad goes to a second room from the first time to the second time
    and the room is different from the second room.
